#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175970 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d646eda-468b-5ae5-b7b0-0d53a948a808" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing an Elliptic Cylinder"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175972 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87b371d9-6172-5ac0-a105-c9887542f0a7" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Parabolic Cylinder"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175973 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a36a5f81-d625-5eff-af81-12f7d91e02bb" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Hyperbolic Cylinder"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176984 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will explore strategies for parametrizing cylinders. \n\nParametrizing a cylinder is no more difficult than parametrizing its base curve. The only difference is that we need a *second* parameter to control the variation parallel to the cylinder's axis. This second parameter is usually allowed to vary arbitrarily. \n\nFor example, consider the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\ny = 8x^2.\n$$\n\nNotice that $y = y(x)$ is an explicit function of $x.$ Therefore, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on this surface, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,8{x}^{2},\\,z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! We have successfully found a parametrization of this cylinder. \n\nNote that, under this parametrization\n\n- the parameter $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ controls the movement along the base curve, and\n\n- the parameter $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ controls the movement parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nLet's see some more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "113dbe3c-fdc0-5e8b-886e-903d9a42ef3d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5307 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177077 :tutorial/content "Let's find a parametrization of the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{25}+\\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1.\n$$\n\nThe base curve in the $xz$-plane is an ellipse centered at $O$ with semi-axes of lengths $\\sqrt{25} = 5$ and $\\sqrt{16} = 4.$ Therefore, we can parametrize the base curve in the $xz$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\langle x,z \\rangle = \\big\\langle 5\\cos{t}, \\: 4\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nNote that the axis of the cylinder coincides with the $y$-axis. Therefore, the parameter $y$ is allowed to vary arbitrarily." :tutorial/id #uuid "34189953-4c0f-588f-ab1c-6066d0b98e2f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7853 :tutorial/title "Elliptic Cylinders"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177078 :tutorial/content "To find a parametrization of one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{25}-\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1\n$$\n\nfor $x \\geq 5,$ recall that the corresponding hyperbola in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x,y \\rangle = \\big\\langle 5 \\sec{t}, \\: 4 \\tan{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\\qquad t\\in\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2,\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)\n$$\n\nWe can check that this indeed parametrizes our base hyperbola by substituting the parametric equations into the equation of the base curve: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2,\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b77aec62-7485-5124-b4df-403bfa5dd1f8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7854 :tutorial/title "Hyperbolic Cylinders"}]}
